  "market_context": "Uptrend scenario with support level and engulfing candle entry; analyst uses hypothetical $1,000 account to illustrate capital preservation under consecutive losses.",
  "key_observation": "Five consecutive 5% losses erase 25% of capital, while five 2% losses only erase 10%, significantly improving recovery potential and psychological resilience.",
  "analysis_steps": [
    "Step 1: Identify a long setup at support with an engulfing candle entry and stop-loss placed below a doji candle.",
    "Step 2: Calculate loss for a 5% risk trade: $50 on $1,000 account; five consecutive losses = $250 (25% drawdown).",
    "Step 3: Compare with 2% risk trade: $20 per loss; five consecutive losses = $100 (10% drawdown), emphasizing easier recovery and mental stability.",
    "Step 4: Introduce partial position exit in losing trades to save 25% of risk, and profit-taking before final target to protect winning trades."
  ],
  "outcome": null,
  "lessons": "Proper risk-per-trade sizing (e.g., 2% vs 5%) dramatically reduces drawdown severity during losing streaks, preserving capital and trader psychology for long-term survival.",
